{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head 9f30db4245ea2831297de51840343e9e3699b690, artifact 4f23a8fa0a3de04dec05fd37addf517ac07ea4ec.\n\nBlocker: the widening rests on a false model of replacement. The current charter b86860f2 does not cite the predecessor charter it says it replaces. Its only direct basis is request 0fe20fcc, and it is stale because that request was retired. A successor does not have to rest on the predecessor: the successor can rest on stable current governance, while the separate supersession act rests first on the old charter and can name the successor as its additional basis. That is the same separation this repository now requires for artifact succession. Correct replacement therefore does not make the new charter stale by construction.\n\nSecurity: accepting a stale charter converts a flare into continuing authority for both observation and the irreversible public propose operation. The moved basis might be a withdrawn scope request, retired governance, or authority that no longer holds; printing a warning after admission does not re-authorize it and requires no operator acknowledgement. The clause side compounds this: ClausesFrom still does not establish that an admitted clause rests on the --charter chosen for the run, so a stale operator clause can be reused beneath another charter. Current operator role on the clause author is not a substitute for the clause's signed basis.\n\nKeep the proposal coherence check's stale request/artifact refusal; it is correct for exactly this reason. Apply the same fail-closed currentness rule at the charter and clause boundaries. Retain the useful named-refusal diagnostics from this branch. Then state a fresh scoped outbound charter on a stable current basis, ratify it, retire the old charter with a supersession that links old and new, and state a fresh clause resting on the new charter. That is both safer and simpler than two liveness semantics plus warning channels.\n\nThe tests still do not prove the live governance shape. The stale-charter test flips a boolean on an isolated Statement; it does not construct the actual causal chain or prove a replacement can be made current. There is no command-level test that binds the selected charter to the clause that admits an issue, nor an adversarial test where the clause's own charter is retired and a different live charter is supplied. Add those if this design is retained; under the simpler fail-closed repair, test the fresh successor and mismatched-charter refusal instead.\n\nArchitecture: affected layers are the Workroom application projection and GitHub connector/CLI. The kernel remains semantic-free and the documented layer contract is preserved; no architecture-page update is needed. Security conclusion is blocking as above. Simplification conclusion: fresh successor governance plus one currentness rule achieves the requested dogfood without changing connector authorization semantics.\n\nVerification passed at this exact head: go test -count=1 ./cmd/gitseq-github ./internal/connector/github; focused go vet; git diff --check against current main.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4f23a8fa0a3de04dec05fd37addf517ac07ea4ec","head":"9f30db4245ea2831297de51840343e9e3699b690","verdict":"changes-requested"}}